"Many kinds of music grew in Taiwan, and people sing them in different languages. Taiwanese folk songs can be sorted by ethnic group and language: songs of the indigenous peoples in their own languages, Hakka folk songs mainly in Hakka, and Hokkien folk songs mainly in Taiwanese Hokkien. The Council of Indigenous Peoples recognizes 16 indigenous peoples officially. In this lesson we ask one question about all of them: how can voices be layered? There are some useful words. In unison, two or more parts sound the same pitch, or pitches an octave apart. In a round, each voice sings the same melody, but each begins at a different time. A drone is a note or chord that is sounded continuously throughout most or all of a piece. Polyphony is a texture of two or more simultaneous lines of independent melody. Heterophony is the simultaneous variation of a single melodic line. Several indigenous peoples of Taiwan are known for polyphonic singing. The Amis, Bunun, Paiwan, Rukai, and Tsou are known for their polyphonic vocals, and each has a unique variety. The Council of Indigenous Peoples says Amis songs use lead-and-answer singing, chanting, and polyphonic counterpoint. The Bunun style called eight-part harmony starts with four parts, and the “eight” comes from overtones that appear at a high register. It is sung on vowels, without words. Some songs belong to a time and a place. According to Chinese Wikipedia, Amis ritual music is mostly sung only at specific ceremonies. The Council of Indigenous Peoples says the sacred ancestral songs of the Thao are not to be sung casually. Taiwan’s 2007 Act for the protection of indigenous traditional intellectual creations lists music and songs, and a right holder may license their use. That is why this lesson describes the ways of singing, and does not copy any song. Han traditions layer sound in other ways. Nanguan is an old kind of music popular in Fujian, Taiwan, and Southeast Asia. The singer holds clappers, and the accompaniment is led by the pipa, with the xiao, sanxian, erxian, and a set of four boards. It is described as typically slow and gentle, and as heterophonic. Beiguan is another Han tradition in Taiwan; its name is paired with Nanguan, and “north” also stands for a lively, high-pitched style. Hakka mountain songs are short in form with free rhythm, and are popular in mountain villages. The main tunes are the old mountain song, the mountain-song-zi, and the pingban. The tunes are fixed, and the words can be changed and improvised. Shange has traditionally been sung while people work, particularly while picking tea. Taiwanese opera developed in Taiwan after folk arts came from southern Fujian, and its tunes were taken from folk songs.": "6e4319c8094f",
